what is rebac

ChatGPT a dit :
ReBAC stands for Relationship-Based Access Control.

It is an access-control model where permissions are derived from relationships between entities, rather than only from roles or attributes.

Simple definition
ReBAC answers the question:
“Who is allowed to do what, based on how entities are related?”

Instead of saying “User has role X”, ReBAC says:
“User is related to this resource in this way, therefore the action is allowed.”

Why ReBAC exists
Traditional models have limits:

RBAC → too coarse (“admin”, “user”)

ABAC → complex and hard to reason about at scale

ReBAC is designed for:

complex hierarchies

multi-tenant systems

shared resources

delegation and ownership chains

Core concept
ReBAC is built on a graph of relationships:

User ── owner ──► Project ── contains ──► Dataset
Permissions are inferred by walking the relationship graph.

Example
Relationships
user:alice  owner   project:ml-platform
project:ml-platform  contains  dataset:training-data
Policy (conceptual)
A user can read a dataset if:

they are an owner of the project

and the project contains the dataset

Alice can read training-data without being explicitly listed on the dataset.

ReBAC vs other models
Model	Based on	Limitation
RBAC	Roles	Rigid, role explosion
ABAC	Attributes	Complex, implicit
ReBAC	Relationships	Explicit, scalable
OpenFGA and ReBAC
OpenFGA is a ReBAC engine.

It implements:

relationship tuples

permission inference

graph traversal

fine-grained authorization

Example in OpenFGA terms:

user:alice#member@project:ml-platform
project:ml-platform#dataset@dataset:training-data
Then:

can user:alice read dataset:training-data ?
In your architecture (important)
ReBAC is used only for “who can do what”:

Can this user access memory?

Can this agent call this tool?

Can this service read this dataset?

It does NOT:

enforce guardrails

evaluate content

apply compliance logic

That is OPA’s job.

One-sentence summary
ReBAC controls access by modeling relationships, not roles—making it ideal for complex, multi-tenant AI platforms.

